## 🧮 Mathematical Foundations

### Bayes Theorem

**Basic Form:**
```
P(Y | X) = P(X | Y) × P(Y) / P(X)

where:
Y: Class/label
X: Features
P(Y | X): Posterior (what we want)
P(X | Y): Likelihood (probability of features given class)
P(Y): Prior (prior probability of class)
P(X): Evidence (normalization constant)
```

**For Classification:**
```
For multiple classes C₁, C₂, ..., Cₖ:

Predict: argmax_c P(c | x) = argmax_c [P(x | c) × P(c)] / P(x)

P(x) same for all classes, so:

Predict: argmax_c [P(x | c) × P(c)]
         = argmax_c [Likelihood × Prior]
```

### Naive Independence Assumption

**Assumption:**
```
Given the class, features are independent

P(X₁, X₂, ..., Xₙ | Y) = ∏ᵢ P(Xᵢ | Y)
                         = P(X₁|Y) × P(X₂|Y) × ... × P(Xₙ|Y)

This is "naive" because rarely true in practice
But works surprisingly well in practice!
```

**Why It Works:**
```
1. Reduces complexity: O(n²) → O(n)
2. Less data needed: O(2ⁿ) → O(n) parameters
3. Errors partially cancel out
4. Simple model generalizes well
```

### Posterior Probability

```
P(Y=c | X) ∝ P(Y=c) × ∏ᵢ P(Xᵢ | Y=c)

Taking log (easier numerically):
log P(Y=c | X) = log P(Y=c) + Σᵢ log P(Xᵢ | Y=c)

Decision rule:
Classify as: argmax_c [log P(c) + Σᵢ log P(xᵢ | c)]
```

